    title: 'Map symbols and definitions to recall',
    body: '- Contour: a line joining places of equal height above sea level; closely spaced contours mean a steep slope\n- Hachures: short lines drawn to show the direction and steepness of a slope\n- Relief: the difference between the highest and lowest points in an area\n- Blue = water features, brown = contours, black = buildings and railways, green = vegetation, red = main roads\n- The legend or key explains the symbols; the north arrow or compass rose gives direction\n- A great circle is the shortest distance between two points on the Earth surface\n- Cartography is the art and science of making maps; a cadastral survey fixes land boundaries\n- Thematic map shows one feature, such as rainfall; topographical map shows relief and features\n- Remote sensing gathers information without physical contact; aerial photographs are taken from aircraft\n- GIS stores, analyses and displays geographically referenced data; GPS is the Global Positioning System\n- Pixel is one cell of a digital image; vector data are points, lines and polygons; raster data are a grid of cells\n- A geodetic datum is the reference figure of the Earth used to compute positions\n- Geological maps are used in mineral and groundwater exploration; sheet erosion is the removal of thin layers of topsoil by running water\n- Hinterland is the commercial region served by a port; a trade surplus means exports exceed imports'
